Below is a minimal example of a “rotating card” that spins around its Y‑axis when you hover over it.  
Feel free to copy‑paste it into an HTML file and tweak the dimensions, colors or animation speed to fit your design.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="pl">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Rotating Card Demo</title>
<style>
/* Container that keeps the perspective */
.card-wrapper {
  width: 300px;
  height: 200px;
  margin: 50px auto;
  perspective: 1000px;          /* Gives a 3‑D feel */
}

/* The card itself */
.card {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  background: #fff;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 10px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.2);
  transition: transform .6s ease;   /* Smooth rotation */
  transform-style: preserve-3d;     /* Needed for 3‑D transforms */
  cursor: pointer;
}

/* Hover effect – rotate 180° around Y axis */
.card:hover {
  transform: rotateY(180deg);
}

/* Optional: add a back face so you can see something on the reverse side */
.card::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: #f0f0f0;
  border-radius: 12px;
  backface-visibility: hidden;   /* Hide when facing away */
  transform: rotateY(180deg);     /* Place it behind the front face */
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="card-wrapper">
  <div class="card">
    <!-- Front content -->
    <h2 style="margin:0;padding:1rem;">Front Side</h2>
  </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

| Element | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `.card-wrapper` | Sets the 3‑D perspective for the whole card. |
| `.card` | The card itself; `transform-style: preserve-3d` allows child elements to keep their 3‑D orientation. |
| `transition: transform .6s ease` | Makes the rotation smooth. |
| `transform: rotateY(180deg)` on hover | Rotates the card 180° around the Y‑axis. |
| `backface-visibility: hidden` | Hides the back side when it’s not facing the viewer. |

You can replace the `<h2>` with any content you like (images, text, icons, etc.). If you want the card to flip back to its original position when the mouse leaves, the same CSS already handles that because the `:hover` rule only applies while the cursor is over the element.
